{
 "assessment": "The change adds a new solution.sh script using `head` and `tail` to extract line ranges from stdin. The implementation incorrectly calculates the number of lines for `tail` as `$((end - start))`, which is one less than the inclusive count required, causing the output window to shift forward by one index.",
 "findings": [
  {
   "actual": "l3",
   "at": "solution.sh:line 6",
   "call": "printf 'l1\\nl2\\nl3\\n' | bash solution.sh 2 3",
   "defect": "The pipeline calculates the tail length as $((end - start)) instead of the inclusive count ($((end - start + 1))). This results in extracting one fewer line than requested and starting from START+1 rather than START.",
   "expected": "l2\\nl3"
  },
  {
   "actual": "(empty)",
   "at": "solution.sh:line 6",
   "call": "printf 'l1\\nl2\\n' | bash solution.sh 2 2",
   "defect": "When START equals END, the length calculation yields zero (end - start == 0). The command effectively executes `tail -n 0`, which produces no output even though a single line should be returned.",
   "expected": "l2"
  },
  {
   "actual": "l1\\nl2",
   "at": "solution.sh:line 6",
   "call": "printf 'l1\\nl2\\n' | bash solution.sh 2 5",
   "defect": "When the requested END exceeds input length, `tail -n K` prints all available lines if asked for more than exist. This causes it to ignore the START index and print preceding lines (line 1) that should have been filtered out.",
   "expected": "l2"
  }
 ]
}
